From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:10:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] canbus: Use unsigned CAN identifier flag constants

CANMSG_ID_EFF was formed by shifting the signed integer constant 1 into bit 31.  The C language does not define a signed left shift whose result is not representable as an int, so compiler optimizations were not required to retain the intended 0x80000000 mask.

Give both identifier flag constants an unsigned type.  This matches the uint32_t field that stores the flags and makes every backend extended-frame test well-defined without changing the wire representation.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/src/generic/canbus.h b/src/generic/canbus.h
index a0a2c2ab..c280bf68 100644
--- a/src/generic/canbus.h
+++ b/src/generic/canbus.h
@@ -12,8 +12,15 @@ struct canbus_msg {
     };
 };
 
-#define CANMSG_ID_RTR (1<<30)
-#define CANMSG_ID_EFF (1<<31)
+/*
+ * CAN identifiers and their SocketCAN-compatible flags are stored together in
+ * a uint32_t.  Keep the flag constants unsigned as well: shifting a signed
+ * integer into bit 31 is undefined by C, so an optimizer would not be required
+ * to preserve the extended-frame test even though common compilers currently
+ * produce the intended 0x80000000 value.
+ */
+#define CANMSG_ID_RTR (1u<<30)
+#define CANMSG_ID_EFF (1u<<31)
 
 #define CANMSG_DATA_LEN(msg) ((msg)->dlc > 8 ? 8 : (msg)->dlc)
 
